{{Culture of Balisca}}

'''Immigration to Balisca''' is the process by which people [[w:human migration|migrate]] to [[Balisca]] for the purpose of residing there—and where a majority go on to become [[Baliscans|Baliscan citizens]]. In absolute numbers, Balisca has the second largest immigrant population in the world, numbering 35,238,869 people in the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]]. This represents 22.8% of Balisca's population. Also current to 2025, there were approximately 58 million immigrants and Baliscan-born children of immigrants residing in the country, accounting for 37.3% of the overall population.

Immigration has been a very important demographic factor in the composition, structure and history of human population in Balisca, with all its attending factors and consequences in culture, economy, education and [[Baliscan values|social values]]. Despite its many challenges, Balisca gradually [[Social Development Index|developed]] a [[W:Polyculturalism|polycultural]] and [[W:Polyethnicity|polyethnic]] society. Since the progressive reforms of the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]], Balisca has maintained universal {{W|birthright citizenship}}, making every child born on Baliscan soil a Baliscan national with no regards to their parent’s origin. Additional reforms in the 1880s following the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' expanded the scope of this clause; children born to at least one Baliscan parent on foreign land was also considered a Baliscan national, allowing them all benefits of Baliscan citizenships. As a result of these liberal policies Balisca welcomed millions of immigrants that were subjected to racist {{W|nativism|nativist}} discrimination in {{W|Western world|Western countries}}. This has resulted in high levels of {{W|chain migration}} and the emergence of {{W|ethnic enclaves}}.

Balisca's immigration policy is based on the concept of [[W:Free migration|free migration]] in accordance with Article 13 of the [[W:Universal Declaration of Human Rights|Universal Declaration of Human Rights]]. As a result, people migrating to Balisca are not divided into categories (such as refugee, alien, asylum seeker, economic migrant and "illegal" immigrant) instead using ‘immigrant’ for all.

Immigration is the main driver of population growth in Balisca, driven mainly by [[Socioeconomic impact of immigration to Balisca|socioeconomic policy]], {{W|humanitarianism|humanitarian concerns}} and also {{W|family reunification}}. One million immigrants were admitted to Balisca in 2021, with annual immigration numbers projected to surpass 1.5 million people per year by 2026. New immigrants are relatively evenly split between major urban areas and their {{W|satellite city|satellites}} (such as [[Kimünrayen]], [[Axarquía]], [[Yerba Buena]] and [[Castejón]]), and [[rural society in Balisca|Rural Balisca]], with it's emerging cities (such as [[Uxiu]], [[Baccara]], [[Solís]] and [[Las Alemáns]]).

